{"ts": 1790266017.331, "event": "plan_created", "payload": {"plan": {"hypothesis": "The checkout latency spike is caused by a transient resource bottleneck (CPU/memory) or a misconfigured autoscaling policy in the order-service cluster, rather than a code defect in the checkout flow.", "steps": [{"step_id": "S1", "objective": "Verify cluster resource utilization and autoscaling metrics", "success_signal": "CPU and memory usage remain below 70% and the HPA scales to the expected replica count within 2 minutes.", "status": "pending"}, {"step_id": "S2", "objective": "Inspect order-service pod logs for OOM or throttling events", "success_signal": "No OOMKilled or CPU throttling warnings in the last 5 minutes.", "status": "pending"}, {"step_id": "S3", "objective": "Validate request queue lengths and latency distribution in the load balancer", "success_signal": "Queue lengths stay below 10 requests and p95 latency drops below 800ms.", "status": "pending"}, {"step_id": "S4", "objective": "Temporarily increase replica count to 2× current minimum", "success_signal": "Checkout success rate rises above 99% and latency stabilizes.", "status": "pending"}, {"step_id": "S5", "objective": "Confirm autoscaling thresholds and adjust if necessary", "success_signal": "HPA triggers scaling before 80% CPU usage and maintains healthy replica count during peak.", "status": "pending"}, {"step_id": "S6", "objective": "Deploy a canary test with synthetic traffic to reproduce the surge", "success_signal": "Synthetic traffic shows latency within SLO and no errors.", "status": "pending"}, {"step_id": "S7", "objective": "Rollback any recent configuration changes that may affect scaling", "success_signal": "Post-rollback metrics match pre-incident baseline.", "status": "pending"}, {"step_id": "S8", "objective": "Document findings and update incident playbook", "success_signal": "Incident report completed and playbook updated with new autoscaling guidelines.", "status": "pending"}], "revision": 0, "rationale_summary": "The incident was triggered during a traffic surge, suggesting that the system may not have scaled appropriately. A sudden increase in latency and error rate can result from insufficient pod replicas, throttled CPU, or memory pressure, leading to request queuing and timeouts. A code regression is less likely given the lack of recent deployments and the fact that the issue surfaced only under load."}}}
